WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.670
Hi, my name is Shannon Pileggi.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.440
And here are three
reasons why you

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.410
should take What They Forgot
to Teach You About R: Project-

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:17.190
Oriented Workflows With Git and
GitHub workshop. Number one,

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.800
I'm really excited to co-lead
this workshop with David

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.630
for the second year in a row.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.670
David is a solutions
engineer at Posit,

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.550
and I'm a lead data scientist
with the Prostate Cancer

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.740
Clinical Trials Consortium.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.810
David and I work
really well together

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.250
and we learn from each other.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.780
Our diverse perspectives really
benefit the participants

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.930
in this course.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:40.570
Number two, the
spirit of the 'What They

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.520
Forgot To Teach You
About R' workshop,

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.410
are strategies to
improve the quality

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:47.860
of your R life via
project workflows

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.560
and version control
with Git and GitHub.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.140
These are paradigms that you
can benefit from no matter what

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.500
stage you are in in your career.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:57.580
Number three.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.090
Lastly, the 'What They Forgot
to Teach You About R' content

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.260
is solid.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.340
This workshop has actually
been instructed since 2018,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.430
so the course content has
a really strong foundation

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:12.820
that's assessed every year to
be updated for new developments.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:15.970
So I really hope you can
join us at posit::conf.

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:17.570
Hi, I'm David Aja.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.620
I'm a Solutions Engineer at Posit.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.480
Sometimes I describe
that as a role

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.310
focused on helping
data scientists get

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.590
their ideas into production.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.150
But in practice, what that
means is that a lot of the time,

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:32.830
I'm focused on the
parts of working with

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:35.290
R that aren't about
writing R code.

00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:38.350
But more about what it looks
like to manage it, to share it,

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:41.500
and to help it answer questions
for lots of different user

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.620
groups.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.950
So the reason I think you
should take What They Forgot

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.010
To Teach You About R is I
think it helps - It's

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.560
an opportunity to help you
learn some of the software

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.670
engineering fundamentals
that aren't really

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:58.000
going to come up in other
kinds of R-focused programming

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.110
classes.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.390
I know that when I started
learning R programming,

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.070
I was very much
focused on like, how

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:07.540
do I extract the data
from this HTML table

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.550
and not as focused on
questions like, what is it

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.300
going to look like to
work on this project

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.270
again in another year?

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:15.560
Or what's it going to
look like to collaborate

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.310
with this project on -

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.570
with someone else?

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.428
And I think 'What They Forgot'
is excellent venue for learning

00:02:21.428 --> 00:02:22.220
that kind of thing.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.270
So I hope to see you there.

